.PP
Date arguments accept an ISO date (\f[CR]2026\-06\-10\f[R]),
\f[CR]today\f[R] / \f[CR]tomorrow\f[R] / \f[CR]yesterday\f[R], or a
relative offset (\f[CR]+3d\f[R], \f[CR]\-1d\f[R], \f[CR]+2w\f[R]).
\f[CR]none\f[R]/\f[CR]clear\f[R] removes the field.
.SH Keybinds (defaults, last\-write\-wins)
.IP
.EX
<space>mc   :task_cycle           (acts immediately)
<space>ma   :task_agenda          (acts immediately)
<space>mn   :task_note ▏          (opens the : prompt pre\-filled)
<space>md   :task_deadline ▏
<space>ms   :task_schedule ▏
<space>mp   :task_prio ▏
<space>mx   :task_archive         (acts immediately)
<space>mX   :task_archive_done    (acts immediately)
<space>X    :task_capture ▏       (global, not markdown\-only)
.EE
.PP
The argument\-taking commands open the \f[CR]:\f[R] prompt pre\-filled
with the command + a trailing space, so you land ready to type the value
(or press Enter for the command\[cq]s empty\-arg default \[em]
e.g.\ \f[CR]:task_deadline\f[R] with no arg = today).
Override or add binds in \f[CR]src/config.h\f[R].
.SH Capture
\f[CR]<space>X\f[R] (or \f[CR]:task_capture [title]\f[R]) prepends a new
task at the top of the todo file, newest first \[em]
\f[CR]<org root>/todo.md\f[R] by default, overridable with
\f[CR]task_todo_file()\f[R] in config or \f[CR]:task_todo_file\f[R] at
runtime:
.IP
.EX
## [TODO] fix delta parsing
created:: 2026\-08\-19

\f[I][\f[R]parse.c:214\f[I]](/home/me/src/parse.c:214)\f[R]
.EE
.PP
With no title the file:line stands in as the heading, and the heading
level copies the first heading already in the todo file (\f[CR]#\f[R]
for a fresh one).
The reference is a plain markdown link: the highlighter renders it, and
\f[CR]gf\f[R] on the target follows it to the exact line.
If the todo file is open in a buffer the capture lands there (unsaved,
one undo step); otherwise the file on disk is rewritten via a temp file
+ rename.
Either way the editor then jumps to the todo file with the cursor on the
fresh capture (\f[CR]<C\-o>\f[R] / \f[CR]<space>jb\f[R] returns to where
you were).
.PP
Capturing works from any buffer with a name, not just files: in a mail
thread view (mail plugin) the link becomes
\f[CR][<subject>](mail://thread:\&...)\f[R], and \f[CR]gf\f[R] on it
reopens that thread \[em] mail buffers are temporary, but the thread id
is stable.
.SH Agenda
\f[CR]:task_agenda\f[R] greps the tree for open tasks (via
\f[CR]rg \-l\f[R]), scans each file\[cq]s heading + field block in C,
and builds a quickfix list sorted by deadline (overdue first), then
priority, then title.
Each line is prefixed with \f[CR][#A]\f[R] and a deadline marker
(\f[CR]!OVERDUE Nd\f[R], \f[CR]due today\f[R], \f[CR]due +Nd\f[R]).
Requires \f[CR]rg\f[R] on \f[CR]PATH\f[R].
.SS Org root & blacklist
By default the agenda scans the current working directory.
Point it at a fixed org tree \[em] and exclude files or directories
\[em] from your user config (\f[CR]\[ti]/.config/hed/config.c\f[R]):
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]\[dq]tasks/tasks.h\[dq]\f[R]

void config_user_init(void) {
    task_org_root(\[dq]/home/me/org\[dq]);        \f[I]/* agenda + :org\-files scan here */\f[R]
    task_agenda_ignore(\[dq]archive\[dq]);        \f[I]/* skip dir named archive, anywhere */\f[R]
    task_agenda_ignore(\[dq]drafts/**\[dq]);      \f[I]/* skip a subtree */\f[R]
    task_agenda_ignore(\[dq]*.draft.md\[dq]);     \f[I]/* skip matching files */\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
Globs use gitignore semantics (each becomes
\f[CR]rg \-g \[aq]!<glob>\[aq]\f[R]).
The same blacklist applies to the \f[CR]:org\-files\f[R] picker.
At runtime, \f[CR]:task_agenda_ignore <glob>\f[R] adds a session\-only
entry, no argument lists the current set, and \f[CR]clear\f[R] resets
it; \f[CR]:task_org_root\f[R] likewise sets/shows/clears the root.
\f[CR]:task_agenda <path>\f[R] scans an explicit path, overriding the
configured root.
.SH Archival
Archiving moves a whole task section \[em] heading + field block + prose
+ log + any sub\-tasks \[em] out of \f[CR]foo.md\f[R] and appends it to
the sibling \f[CR]foo.md_archive\f[R], stamping
\f[CR]archived:: <date>\f[R] first.
Sections in the archive are separated by a blank line, and the archive
is itself a valid task markdown file (highlighting and
\f[CR]:task_*\f[R] commands work there too; \f[CR]:task_archive\f[R]
refuses to run \f[I]on\f[R] an archive file).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]:task_archive\f[R] (\f[CR]<space>mx\f[R]) \[em] the section under
the cursor.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]:task_archive_done\f[R] (\f[CR]<space>mX\f[R]) \[em] sweep every
closed task.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]:task_archive_open\f[R] \[em] jump to the archive.
.PP
Archiving is a durable move: the source buffer is \f[B]saved\f[R]
afterward so disk and buffer never disagree.
The write is ordered archive\-first \[em] if appending to
\f[CR]_archive\f[R] fails, nothing is removed from the source.
.SH Notes & limitations
.IP \[bu] 2
The cursor can sit on the heading \f[I]or\f[R] anywhere in its
field/prose block \[em] commands walk up to the nearest heading.
.IP \[bu] 2
Heading/field detection does not track fenced code blocks, so a
\f[CR]# [TODO]\f[R] or \f[CR]key:: v\f[R] line inside a \[ga]\[ga]\[ga]
fence is also recognized.
The conventions make that rare.
